The relationship between counting systems and the development of high number mathematics is foundational, as counting systems evolved from simple graphical representations of small numbers to complex numeral systems that enable the manipulation of large numbers and abstract mathematical concepts. Early counting systems, such as those based on grouping (e.g., base-10), provided the structural framework for arithmetic and later algebraic operations [[6](http://alumni.media.mit.edu/~stefanm/society/som_final.html)]. These systems allowed societies to transition from concrete counting to symbolic representation, which is critical for advanced mathematics [[1](https://www.britannica.com/science/numeral)]. 

Counting also underpins number sense, which includes understanding numerical relations, magnitude, and operations—key precursors to formal math instruction [[8](https://mybrainware.com/blog/cognitive-skills-and-math/)]. Cognitive systems like the approximate number system (ANS) and parallel individuation (PI) further support this development by enabling the extraction of numerical information and the distinction of individual items, laying the groundwork for symbolic mathematics [[3](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27747998/)]. 

In early education, counting fosters concepts like place value, decomposition, and equivalence, which are essential for higher mathematics [[9](https://prek-math-te.stanford.edu/counting/mathematics-counting-0)]. The transition from counting as a notational tool to understanding number conservation and abstract operations marks a critical step in mathematical development [[10](https://www.jstor.org/stable/1129054)]. 

